Let $T_0 = 50$ be the initial number of trees Tom has.
Each year, Tom plants 10 trees and chops down 2 trees. So the net increase in the number of trees is $10 - 2 = 8$ trees per year.
Therefore, after $n$ years, the number of trees Tom has is given by $T_n = T_0 + 8n$.
In this case, we want to find the number of trees after 10 years, so $n = 10$.
$T_{10} = 50 + 8(10) = 50 + 80 = 130$ trees.
After 10 years, 30% of the trees die. This means that $0.30 \times 130 = 39$ trees die.
So the number of trees left is $130 - 39 = 91$ trees.

Alternatively, if 30% of the trees die, then 70% of the trees remain.
So the number of trees left is $0.70 \times 130 = 91$ trees.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{91}$
